<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Haguenau</h1><p class="st-meta">1260 CE – 1679 CE (approx.) · capital Haguenau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Alsatian imperial city grown around Barbarossa's favourite palace, seat of the imperial bailiff and leading city of the Decapolis until France took it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Haguenau grew beside a castle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> dukes on an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moder">Moder</a>, in the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haguenau-forest">Haguenau Forest</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> granted it a charter in 1164 and rebuilt the castle as an imperial palace where the regalia were kept. After the Hohenstaufen fell, it was established as an imperial city by around 1260.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Haguenau was seat of the imperial bailiff of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and, from 1354, the leading member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/decapolis">Decapolis</a>, the league of ten Alsatian imperial cities. A council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> ran its affairs, with trade, forestry and crafts as its economy. In the early 16th century it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> centre. The city remained largely Catholic.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> rights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, and French troops burned Haguenau in 1677; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> in 1679 confirmed French rule. It is now a town in Alsace, France.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1260 CE – 1679 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.78%;width:8.34%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1260 CE – 1679 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Haguenau</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alsatian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Haguenau, Hagenau</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1470&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-haguenau" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Haguenau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4860 473 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Haguenau lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5180 729L5918 1245M5180 729L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5918 1245h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5180 729h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5180 729h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Haguenau. <a href="/atlas?year=1470&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-haguenau">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

